.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt APREFCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm aprefctl
.Nd control file prefetch and look at its records
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm status
.Nm
.Cm record Cm start | stop
.Nm
.Cm boot-done
.Nm
.Cm mark
.Ar label ...
.Nm
.Cm marks
.Op Fl s
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Cm dump
.Ar profile
.Nm
.Cm delete
.Ar profile
.Nm
.Cm trace
.Op Ar file
.Nm
.Cm scatter
.Op Fl a
.Op Fl c Ar csvfile
.Op Fl s Ar svgfile
.Op Ar file
.Nm
.Cm replay
.Op Fl b Ar bytes
.Op Fl w Ar seconds
.Ar profile
.Nm
.Cm replay-stop
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
controls
.Xr aprefetch 4
and shows what it recorded.
A
.Ar profile
is a path, or
.Cm boot
for
.Pa /etc/aprefetch/boot.prof ;
the profiles of login sessions are in
.Pa /var/db/aprefetch/session .
A trace
.Ar file
is
.Pa /var/db/aprefetch/boot.trace
unless given.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm status
Show the settings, whether a recording window is open and a replay is
running, and the statistics.
.It Cm record Cm start | stop
Open or close a recording window by hand.
.It Cm boot-done
Close the boot recording window.
It is run at each login through
.Xr pam_exec 8
from
.Pa /etc/pam.d/login ,
does nothing when the window is closed already, and says nothing when
.Pa /dev/aprefetch
does not exist.
A display manager should run it when it shows its login screen, so that
the time spent typing a password is not recorded, or else when someone
has logged in.
.It Cm mark Ar label ...
Add a mark.
.It Cm marks Op Fl s
Show the marks with the time since boot, the time since the previous
mark, and the disk reads in between, followed by a summary: the time
from boot to the root mount, the time and reads from the start of init to
the end of
.Pa /etc/rc ,
and the slowest
.Xr rc.d 8
scripts.
With
.Fl s ,
show the summary only.
.It Cm list
List the profiles and then the traces, first those of the boot and then
those of the login sessions.
.It Cm dump Ar profile
Show the header and the entries of a profile.
The kind of an entry is
.Dq data
for a range of a file,
.Dq dir
for a block of a directory or symbolic link, and
.Dq dev
for a block of the device of a file system, such as one that holds
inodes; for the latter the offset is the block number.
.It Cm delete Ar profile
Remove a profile.
.It Cm trace Op Ar file
Show the entries of a trace in time order, with their kinds as for
.Cm dump .
.It Cm scatter Oo Fl a Oc Oo Fl c Ar csvfile Oc Oo Fl s Ar svgfile Oc Op Ar file
Map the entries of a trace that missed the cache, or all entries with
.Fl a ,
to disk blocks, metadata included, and show how scattered the reads were:
the number of jumps between consecutive pieces on the same disk and their
average size, once
in the order of the reads and once in the order of the disk, followed by a
plot of disk position against time.
.Fl c
also writes the pieces to
.Ar csvfile
and
.Fl s
draws the plot into
.Ar svgfile .
Entries whose file is gone or has changed are counted but not mapped.
.It Cm replay Oo Fl b Ar bytes Oc Oo Fl w Ar seconds Oc Ar profile
Replay a profile now, reading at most
.Ar bytes ,
which may end in k, m or g, or
.Va kern.aprefetch.app_budget .
As with the boot profile, the file must be owned by root and writable by
nobody else.
Only one replay runs at a time.
With
.Fl w ,
if one is running, try again for up to
.Ar seconds
before failing, and give up as soon as another
.Cm replay
or a
.Cm replay-stop
comes.
.It Cm replay-stop
Stop the replay that is running, and the replays waiting with
.Fl w ,
and wait until the running one has stopped, that is until its reads
are over and its files are released.
It is not an error if no replay is running.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /var/run/aprefctl.replay -compact
.It Pa /etc/aprefetch/boot.prof
the boot profile
.It Pa /var/db/aprefetch
the traces of the boot
.It Pa /var/db/aprefetch/session
the traces and profiles of login sessions
.It Pa /var/run/aprefctl.replay
the process ID of the latest
.Cm replay
or
.Cm replay-stop ,
which a
.Cm replay
waiting with
.Fl w
looks at
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Show how long the kernel and userland parts of the last boot took, and
which
.Xr rc.d 8
scripts were the slowest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# aprefctl marks -s
.Ed
.Pp
See how scattered the reads of the last boot were, and draw them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# aprefctl scatter -s /tmp/boot.svg
.Ed
.Pp
Replay the profile of the session
.Dq fluxbox
of user ID 1000 once the boot replay is over, reading at most 128 MB:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# cd /var/db/aprefetch/session
# aprefctl replay -w 30 -b 128m 1000-fluxbox.prof
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr aprefetch 4 ,
.Xr aprefd 8 ,
.Xr pam_exec 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
was written for AutumnBSD.
